window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["cj5"]=[{"id":"front-bumper-cj5","title":"Steel Front Bumper \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":650},"tools_required":["floor jack","socket set","torque wrench","drill with bits","wire brush","rust penetrant"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC front bumper (CJ-5)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Rampage Products tubular front bumper (CJ-5)","vendor":"Rampage Products","price_estimate_usd":240},{"name":"Warn CJ tube bumper with winch plate","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Frame rail gusset plates (pair)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-5 Front Bumper Fitment Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-bumpers.htm"}],"alternatives":["rock-sliders-cj5","skid-plates-cj5","winch-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5's factory bumper is a thin flat steel bar \u2014 it offers decoration, not protection. A proper steel bumper transforms the front end from a liability into a trail tool.","description":"The CJ-5 front bumper mounts directly to the frame rail ends. The frame rails on a CJ-5 are thin by modern standards \u2014 typically 0.120-inch wall stock from the factory \u2014 and the bumper mounting points take all the impact load. A bumper heavier than about 80 lbs without additional frame support will eventually crack the frame at the mount holes, especially on a rig that sees repeated trail hits.\n\nFor any bumper rated for winch use or weighing more than 80 lbs, weld a gusset plate across both frame rails before mounting. RuffStuff Specialties makes bolt-on frame gusset plates for CJ applications ($85/pair) that don't require welding \u2014 a good option if you don't have access to a welder. The gussets spread the winch and impact load across more of the frame instead of concentrating it at two bolt holes.\n\nThe CJ-5's original bumper is a flat steel bar, which means a flat-face replacement looks factory-correct and mounts cleanly. Smittybilt's SRC flat-face bumper ($280) is a popular bolt-on that maintains the CJ's original look. For a more functional trail build, a tube bumper with a winch mount plate is the right call \u2014 Warn and Rampage Products both make tube-style bumpers for the CJ-5. A tube bumper with a center winch plate positions a winch low and forward, which is the correct placement for line angle on a self-recovery or winch anchor pull.\n\nAvoid heavy full-width rock bumpers designed for full-size trucks \u2014 they are overkill for the CJ-5's weight and will stress the frame rails without gussets.\n\nRemove the factory bumper by unbolting the four mount bolts at the frame rail ends. Most CJ-5s have decades of rust in those threads \u2014 soak with penetrating oil the night before and use a breaker bar. If the bolts are seized, heat with a torch before attempting removal; a snapped bolt in the frame rail is a much bigger problem than the extra hour it takes to free a stuck fastener.\n\nInstall the new bumper with grade-8 hardware (the bumper kit should include it; if not, get 1/2-13 grade-8 bolts). Torque to 50\u201360 ft-lbs. If you are installing a winch at the same time, fit the winch to the bumper before mounting the bumper to the frame \u2014 fighting a loaded winch into position is much harder with the bumper already bolted on."},{"id":"rear-bumper-cj5","title":"Rear Bumper \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":480},"tools_required":["floor jack","socket set","torque wrench","drill with bits","rust penetrant"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear","spare-tire","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC rear bumper (CJ-5)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Rampage Products rear bumper with recovery loops (CJ-5)","vendor":"Rampage Products","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Hood-mount spare tire bracket (CJ-5)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Tailgate-mount spare tire bracket (CJ-5)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-5 Rear Bumper and Spare Tire Options \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-rear-bumper.htm"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-cj5","rock-sliders-cj5","recovery-gear-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5 doesn't have the rear frame overhang needed for a full swing-out tire carrier \u2014 the rear body mounts essentially at the frame end, so a simple bumper with two solid recovery loops is the correct approach.","description":"Before buying a rear bumper for a CJ-5, you need a plan for the spare tire. The short wheelbase means the rear frame ends are directly under the rear body tub \u2014 there is no overhang to support a swing-out carrier without major frame extension fabrication. This is a real design constraint, not a limitation of the aftermarket.\n\nThe practical spare tire solutions for a CJ-5 are: (1) hood mount \u2014 a spare tire bracket bolted to the hood or fender gives you accessible, front-weighted spare carry; (2) tailgate mount \u2014 a carrier bolted to the rear tailgate that swings with the gate; or (3) interior carry on a flat floor behind the seat. For a trail build running 33-inch tires, a hood-mount spare is the most common and most functional choice. The hood can handle a 33x12.5 tire with the right bracket and stays centered over the front axle, keeping weight forward.\n\nKeep it simple: a flat steel bumper with two solid recovery loops (rated at a minimum 2:1 the CJ's weight, so 6,000 lbs per loop minimum). The bumper mounts to the rear frame ends via the factory holes or added brackets. Smittybilt and Rampage Products both make CJ-5-specific units in the $220\u2013$260 range that bolt on with minimal modification.\n\nDon't add a heavy swing-out to a CJ-5 without first extending the frame and welding a proper swing-out hinge gusset \u2014 the frame rails are not designed to handle the moment arm of a loaded swing-out tire carrier, and the mounting geometry is wrong for a standard carrier design.\n\nBoth rear recovery loops on the bumper must be rated for a snatch strap pull \u2014 dynamic recovery loads can reach 2\u20133x the vehicle weight instantaneously. A decorative loop that looks the part but is only welded at one point will tear off under a hard snatch. Confirm that any bumper you buy has recovery loops that are fully seam-welded to a main bumper tube, not tack-welded to a flange."},{"id":"rock-sliders-cj5","title":"Rock Sliders \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":750},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","angle grinder","wire brush"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","basic fabrication helpful"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["armor","rock-sliders","body-protection","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Affordable Offroad CJ-5 tube sliders (pair)","vendor":"Affordable Offroad","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Poison Spyder CJ body protection sliders (pair)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Custom DOM tube slider kit (materials only)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep CJ Rock Slider Options \u2014 Pirate4x4 Tech","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/jeep-cj-sliders/"}],"alternatives":["skid-plates-cj5","front-bumper-cj5","rear-bumper-cj5"],"summary":"A CJ-5 without rock sliders has no barrier between trail rocks and the thin sheet metal of the body tub \u2014 one rock ledge contact at low speed will crease the side in a way that takes serious bodywork to fix.","description":"The CJ-5 body tub is stamped steel, thin by design, and has no structural rocker panel. The flat sides of the tub are the first thing to contact a rock ledge when you're threading through a technical section or sliding off a boulder. The damage is fast, visible, and demoralizing \u2014 a single off-camber rock rub leaves a crease that takes hours at a body shop to address.\n\nThe CJ-5's 83.5-inch wheelbase is also the reason the rig excels in tight terrain where longer Wranglers cannot go. That means you're going to put the body next to more rocks, not fewer, than a CJ-7 or TJ. Sliders are not optional on a trail CJ-5 \u2014 they're the difference between a rig you use hard and a rig you baby.\n\nFrame-mounted sliders are the only meaningful option on a CJ-5. A body-mounted slider transfers rock impact directly into the thin sheet metal tub, which defeats the purpose. A frame-mounted tube slider bolts to the frame rail through the body tub with backing plates, so rock impact goes through the slider tube into the frame rail, bypassing the body entirely.\n\nMost CJ-5 sliders use 2-inch or 1.75-inch DOM (drawn over mandrel) steel tube in a simple triangulated structure that follows the frame rail outboard. The short wheelbase means the sliders are comparatively short \u2014 18\u201324 inches is typical. That actually makes fabrication easier; a local tube shop can build a set from a material kit for less than a finished retail unit.\n\nBecause the CJ-5 is short, the belly of the rig \u2014 the transfer case, oil pan, and gas tank \u2014 is what contacts the ground on most rock encounters, not the sides. Sliders prevent body damage, but pair them with a belly skid plate for comprehensive protection. See `skid-plates-cj5` for that part of the build.\n\nDrill through the body tub and into the frame at the mount points, use large backing plates on the interior side of the tub (to prevent the bolt from pulling through sheet metal under load), and torque to 45\u201355 ft-lbs. Clean and treat the frame rail surface before bolting up \u2014 sliders trap moisture against the frame and will accelerate rust if installed on a rusty frame without prep. Paint or POR-15 the contact surface before installation."},{"id":"skid-plates-cj5","title":"Skid Plates \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"armor","subcategory":"skids","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":520},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","angle grinder","torque wrench","drill with bits"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","basic fabrication helpful"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["armor","skid-plates","belly-protection","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Omix-ADA CJ-5 transfer case skid plate","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"CJ-5 oil pan skid plate (3/16\" steel)","vendor":"GenRight Off Road","price_estimate_usd":165},{"name":"Full belly skid kit (oil pan, T-case, gas tank)","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":390}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ Undercarriage Protection Guide \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-skid-plates.htm"},{"title":"Jeep CJ Belly Skid Fabrication Notes \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/jeep-cj-skid/"}],"alternatives":["rock-sliders-cj5","front-bumper-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5 has no factory underbody protection of any kind \u2014 the oil pan, transfer case output housing, and fuel tank are all exposed to rock contact at stock ride height.","description":"The CJ-5's underbody is a target-rich environment for rock damage. The oil pan on the AMC 258 or AMC 304 hangs low and slightly forward of center. The Dana 18 or Dana 20 transfer case sits roughly amidships with the output yokes low. The gas tank (8\u201315 gallons depending on year) is at the rear, protected only by the tank straps and thin floor. On a 83.5-inch wheelbase, the rig's center section \u2014 where all of this lives \u2014 is the part that contacts high-centered obstacles, not the ends.\n\nEven mild trail use exposes these components to rock contact. A rock strike to the oil pan at low speed is a trail-ender. A cracked T-case housing on the trail means a very long recovery. Skid plates are not a luxury on a CJ-5 used off-road; they're functional equipment.\n\nA 3/16-inch steel belly skid is the right minimum for trail use. 1/4-inch is heavier but better for rock crawler use where the skid will take deliberate hits. Aluminum skids save weight but dent instead of deflect \u2014 on a CJ-5 where weight savings matter less than robustness, steel is the right call.\n\nThe priority order for coverage is: (1) oil pan, (2) transfer case, (3) fuel tank. Rock Hard 4x4 makes a full belly kit ($390) that covers all three in a modular design \u2014 sections can be installed individually if budget is the constraint. Omix-ADA's T-case skid ($145) is a good stand-alone first buy that protects the most vulnerable mechanical component.\n\nGenRight makes a well-regarded CJ-5 oil pan skid in 3/16-inch steel with mounting tabs that bolt to the frame \u2014 no welding required. This is a good starting point for a builder without welding access. For a dedicated rock crawler, a local metal fab shop can build a custom full belly plate from 3/16-inch AR steel (abrasion resistant) for similar cost to retail kits, and cut to exactly fit your specific CJ's frame dimensions.\n\nThe CJ-5 frame rails have few convenient cross-member mounting points for a belly skid \u2014 most mounting schemes use the existing cross-members with added brackets. The oil pan skid typically mounts forward to the front cross-member and aft to a bracket that bolts to the block or frame. Confirm the mount scheme with your specific kit; bolt-on designs vary. Torque all mounting hardware to 35\u201345 ft-lbs and check after the first trail run \u2014 bolt-on skids vibrate loose if not properly torqued."},{"id":"dana-30-front-cj5","title":"Dana 30 Front Axle \u2014 Service and Locker Options \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"axles","subcategory":"front-axle","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator","ring gear holder","seal driver set"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential service experience"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1977-1983"],"tags":["axles","dana-30","front-axle","locker","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right locker (Dana 30)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 30)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"OX Locker (Dana 30)","vendor":"OX Locker","price_estimate_usd":780},{"name":"Dana 30 master overhaul kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Yukon chromoly front shaft set (Dana 30, CJ)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":210}],"citations":[{"title":"Dana 30 Front Axle Locker Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/dana30/"},{"title":"Powertrax Lock-Right Installation \u2014 Dana 30","url":"https://www.powertrax.com/instructions/dana30/"}],"alternatives":["dana-44-rear-cj5","lockers-cj5","regear-cj5"],"summary":"The late-production CJ-5 (1977\u20131983) got the Dana 30 front axle, which opens the door to the full range of Dana 30 locker options. If your CJ-5 has the earlier Dana 27, address that first.","description":"CJ-5s built before approximately 1972 used a Dana 27 front axle \u2014 a lighter, narrower unit with limited aftermarket locker support. The Dana 27 is not the same as a Dana 30 and does not share axle shafts, lockers, or most components. If your CJ-5 has a Dana 27, the practical path forward is to swap in a Dana 30 housing from a 1977\u20131983 CJ-5 or early XJ Cherokee before trying to add traction control. Dana 27 locker options are essentially non-existent in the current market.\n\nThe Dana 30 was introduced on the CJ-5 in the mid-1970s production run and is the front axle on all 1977\u20131983 CJ-5s. It shares shaft specifications with the XJ Cherokee Dana 30 (HP30, high-pinion), which means aftermarket support is excellent.\n\nThe Powertrax Lock-Right is the budget-friendly choice at $280. It's a \"lunch box\" locker \u2014 installed inside the existing carrier without replacing the housing or removing the ring gear. Installation requires pulling the carrier and swapping out the spider gears, a job you can do in a driveway with basic tools. The Lock-Right delivers full-time locking with a characteristic clicking noise during tight-radius turns. It's effective and inexpensive but not driver-controlled.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker ($680) is a pneumatically activated locker that requires an on-board air system (a compressor, lines, and a solenoid). It locks and unlocks on demand from the cab, which is the right choice if the CJ-5 sees any pavement driving. Locked during technical sections, open on pavement \u2014 best of both worlds. The ARB requires carrier replacement and professional setup of the air system.\n\nThe OX Locker ($780) is a cable-actuated selectable locker \u2014 driver-controlled without requiring air. Good middle ground between the Lock-Right's permanence and the ARB's complexity.\n\nInspect and service the differential before adding a locker. Pull the cover, check bearing preload, check for wear on the pinion head bearing. A locker in a worn differential accelerates the wear. If the unit needs a bearing rebuild, do it now \u2014 a Yukon master overhaul kit ($95) includes everything needed.\n\nAlso consider upgrading to Yukon chromoly front shafts ($210) before adding a locker. The Dana 30's stock shafts are adequate on 33-inch tires without a locker but are a failure point once you're putting full locked torque through the axle on 35s."},{"id":"dana-44-rear-cj5","title":"Dana 44 Rear Axle \u2014 Service and Upgrade \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear-axle","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":950},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator","ring gear holder","seal driver set"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential service experience"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1972-1983"],"tags":["axles","dana-44","rear-axle","locker","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (Dana 44)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 44)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":720},{"name":"Superior Axle chromoly shaft set (CJ Dana 44 rear)","vendor":"Superior Axle & Gear","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Dynatrac chromoly shaft set (Dana 44)","vendor":"Dynatrac","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Yukon ring-and-pinion 4.56 (Dana 44)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":210}],"citations":[{"title":"Dana 44 Rear Axle Service Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/dana44/"},{"title":"CJ Dana 44 Locker Options \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-axle-lockers.htm"}],"alternatives":["dana-30-front-cj5","lockers-cj5","regear-cj5"],"summary":"Most 1972\u20131983 CJ-5s left the factory with a Dana 44 rear axle \u2014 a genuine mid-strength axle that supports real upgrades, not just a placeholder until you buy something else.","description":"Not every CJ-5 has a Dana 44 rear. Late-production (1981\u20131983) base models and some earlier economy trims used a Dana 35 rear, which is considerably weaker. Check your axle: the Dana 44 has a 8.5-inch tall differential cover with 10 ring gear bolts; the Dana 35 has a smaller D-shaped cover with 10 smaller bolts. If you have a Dana 35, budget for an axle swap before adding a locker \u2014 the Dana 35 is a stock-tire, open-differential unit and should not be used with a locker on a trail rig.\n\nThe Dana 44 in a CJ-5 uses a low-pinion configuration and a smaller ring gear than the JK Dana 44 \u2014 treat it as a mid-strength axle appropriate for 33-inch tires and moderate trail use, not 37s and full competition rock crawling.\n\nThe stock Dana 44 axle shafts in a CJ-5 are 1541H steel \u2014 adequate for stock use without a locker. Once you add a locker and start running 33s or larger, the shafts become the failure point. A snapped rear shaft on the trail requires either a recovery call or a trailside rebuild (which you can do if you carry spare shafts \u2014 some CJ trail drivers do exactly this). The better approach is chromoly shafts from Superior Axle & Gear ($340) or Dynatrac ($380) before the locker goes in. These shafts are significantly stronger and should survive the life of the build.\n\nThe Powertrax Lock-Right ($280) is the right budget choice for the Dana 44 rear \u2014 lunch-box installation, no carrier swap, works with the existing open carrier. The characteristic click-and-differentiation on pavement is more pronounced on the rear than the front, but manageable. The ARB Air Locker ($720) requires a carrier swap and an air system but delivers driver-selectable engagement \u2014 the right choice for a rig that sees pavement regularly.\n\nIf you're upsizing to 33s or larger, re-gear both axles at the same time the locker goes in. See `regear-cj5` for gear ratio guidance. The Dana 44 ring gear accepts 4.10 and 4.56 Yukon or Motive sets without modification."},{"id":"cj5-drum-brakes","title":"Front and Rear Drum Brake Adjustment","vehicle":"cj5","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":25},"tools_required":["brake spoon adjuster","flat screwdriver","lug wrench","floor jack","jack stands (4)","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","brake-system understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1955-1975","1976-1983"],"tags":["brakes","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Drum brake hardware kit (front, 11x2)","part_number":"H7062","vendor":"Crown / Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Drum brake hardware kit (rear, 10x2 \u2014 76-83)","part_number":"H7063","vendor":"Crown / Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Wheel cylinder (rear, 10\" brake)","part_number":"WC37025","vendor":"Crown","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ5 drum brake picture/diagram (JeepForum)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/cj5-drum-brake-picture-diagram.2974001/"},{"title":"Jeep CJ Series Brake Parts (Midwest Jeep Willys)","url":"https://midwestjeepwillys.com/cj5-cj7-brake72-76.html"},{"title":"Rear Drum Brake \u2014 CJ (Crown Automotive)","url":"https://www.crownautomotive.net/CJ-Rear-Drum-Brake-Parts.html"}],"alternatives":["cj5-front-disc-conversion"],"summary":"Pre-76 CJ-5 has four-wheel drums (11\"x2\" front and rear); 1976-83 has 11\" disc front and 10\"x2\" drum rear. Adjust by turning the star wheel out until light drag is felt, then back off two to four clicks. Re-adjust every 6,000 miles or when pedal travel grows.","description":"The CJ-5 ran two completely different brake configurations across its production. Before 1976 it was four-wheel drums \u2014 11x2\" front and 11x2\" rear on most years. Starting in 1976, AMC switched to a front disc/rear drum setup, with 11\" rotors up front and 10x2\" drums in the rear. The rear drums are not self-adjusting on most years. They drift out of adjustment as the shoes wear, and the pedal goes lower and lower until somebody runs a stop sign or backs into something.\n\nManual adjustment is the answer. Behind each drum is a star-wheel adjuster \u2014 a small toothed wheel on the bottom of the brake backing plate. Turn it one direction to spread the shoes outward (tighter brake), the other to retract them. The adjustment hole is on the backing plate itself or on the back of the drum, depending on year \u2014 a small rubber plug covers it. Pop the plug, slide a brake spoon or flat screwdriver in, and click the wheel.\n\nThe right amount of adjustment: tight enough that the wheel takes solid effort to spin by hand, but not so tight that it locks. The standard procedure is to tighten until the wheel barely won't turn freely, then back off two to four clicks until the wheel spins with no audible drag. Both sides need the same number of clicks \u2014 otherwise the rig will pull under hard braking.\n\nDrum brakes are honest when they're maintained and dangerous when they aren't. The leading shoe does most of the work in forward stopping; the trailing shoe handles reverse. Pedal that feels low after a few miles of driving \u2014 but pumps up \u2014 is usually wheel-cylinder fluid leaking past the cup, not adjustment. Pull the drum and look for any wet streak or fluid pooling at the cylinder. Wet means replace. Dust means clean and adjust.\n\nThe pre-76 front drums are the weakest single point on a stock CJ-5 brake system. Many owners convert to the front disc setup using a salvage 76-83 Dana 30 spindle/hub or an aftermarket conversion kit. That's a different job \u2014 for now, if you're staying drums, the discipline is regular adjustment.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the wheels, and break the lug nuts loose before jacking.","Lift one corner. Place a jack stand under the axle (rear) or the frame rail (front). Pull the wheel.","Spin the drum by hand. Note the resistance \u2014 that's your baseline.","Find the adjustment plug on the backing plate. Pop it out with a small screwdriver.","Insert the brake spoon. Move the handle up (or down \u2014 depends on which side) to spread the shoes. Click the wheel one notch at a time, spinning the drum after each click to feel the change.","Continue until the drum has noticeable drag, then back off 2\u20134 clicks until it spins free with no audible scrape.","Reinstall the plug. Repeat on the opposite side and match the click count.","For the rear, repeat the same process on both sides. For the front (pre-76), same again \u2014 but pay extra attention to symmetry; uneven front-drum adjustment causes a hard pull.","Lower the rig, road test in a parking lot, apply the brakes at 20 mph straight-line. The rig should stop straight. Pull means uneven adjustment or a sticking caliper/cylinder."],"pros":["Free if hardware is intact \u2014 only takes 60 minutes","Restores firm pedal feel and stopping distance","Catches leaking wheel cylinders before they fail completely"],"cons":["Drum brakes fade under repeated hard use (long downhill, towing) \u2014 discs don't","Pre-76 four-wheel drum CJ-5s are a real safety compromise compared to modern brakes","Adjustment drifts back out of spec in 6,000 miles \u2014 recurring service item"]},{"id":"lockers-cj5","title":"Locker Selection \u2014 Front and Rear \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":1600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential service experience"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1972-1983"],"tags":["drivetrain","lockers","traction","dana-30","dana-44","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (Dana 30 front)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (Dana 44 rear)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 30 front)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 44 rear)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":720},{"name":"ARB portable on-board compressor kit","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Locker Comparison Guide \u2014 Four Wheeler Magazine","url":"https://www.fourwheeler.com/how-to/locker-guide/"},{"title":"CJ Dana 30/44 Locker Options \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/lockers/"}],"alternatives":["dana-30-front-cj5","dana-44-rear-cj5","roll-cage-safety-cj5"],"summary":"A locked CJ-5 can climb rocks a JK with open diffs would never attempt \u2014 and because of the 83.5-inch wheelbase, it can access terrain the JK physically cannot reach. That is exactly the problem.","description":"A front-and-rear locked CJ-5 is one of the most capable purpose-built rock crawlers ever made. The short wheelbase allows it to fit into slot canyons and chutes that defeat longer vehicles. Full lockers mean the rig goes where the driver points it regardless of which wheels are in the air. This is not exaggeration \u2014 it's why CJ-5s have been competitive in rock crawling events for decades.\n\nThe flip side: a short-wheelbase rig with a high center of gravity and both axles locked will point straight toward obstacles that require going off-camber to clear. On steep, off-camber terrain \u2014 the exact terrain where lockers are most useful \u2014 the CJ-5 is also most likely to roll. A full cage is not optional if you are locking both ends and running trails seriously. See `roll-cage-safety-cj5`.\n\n**Front (Dana 30):** The Powertrax Lock-Right ($280) is the lunch-box budget choice \u2014 installed without carrier replacement. The ARB Air Locker ($680) is the driver-selectable premium option. On the Dana 30, the Lock-Right's constant engagement is less problematic than it is on a rear axle \u2014 front steering lockers are already disengaged in 2WD.\n\n**Rear (Dana 44):** Same options. For a trail rig that sees pavement occasionally, the ARB selectable locker is worth the premium \u2014 driving with a locked rear on pavement accelerates tire wear and strains the driveline. The Lock-Right disengages on pavement turns (it ratchets), but it's less smooth than a true open differential.\n\nFor the rear Dana 44, install chromoly axle shafts (Superior Axle or Dynatrac, $340\u2013380) before adding a locker. The stock 1541H shafts are a failure point under full locked torque, especially with tires 33 inches or larger.\n\nA front locker on a CJ-5 with the factory manual steering is a pronounced change in steering feel \u2014 on rough terrain, the locked front axle generates significant steering feedback through the steering wheel. This is manageable with a tight steering box (see `steering-box-cj5`) and heavy-duty steering linkage. Do not add a front locker on a CJ-5 with worn steering components \u2014 the combination of locked feedback plus loose steering is dangerous.\n\nRecommended sequence: (1) install rear locker with chromoly shafts, (2) upgrade steering box and tie rod/drag link, (3) build or verify roll cage, (4) install front locker. This order ensures you have the most common (rear) traction device in place before the safety-critical front locker goes in."},{"id":"regear-cj5","title":"Re-Gearing for Bigger Tires \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"gearing","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator","ring gear holder","pinion depth tool","marking compound"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential setup experience \u2014 or take it to a shop"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1972-1983"],"tags":["drivetrain","gearing","regear","dana-30","dana-44","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon 4.56 ring-and-pinion set (Dana 30)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Yukon 4.56 ring-and-pinion set (Dana 44)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":210},{"name":"Motive Gear 4.10 set (Dana 30)","vendor":"Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Motive Gear 4.10 set (Dana 44)","vendor":"Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Dana 30 master install kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"Dana 44 master install kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"Gear Ratio Calculator for Tire Size \u2014 Randy's Ring & Pinion","url":"https://www.ringpinion.com/gear-ratio-calculator/"},{"title":"Jeep CJ Re-Gearing Guide \u2014 Four Wheeler","url":"https://www.fourwheeler.com/how-to/regear-jeep-cj/"}],"alternatives":["lockers-cj5","dana-44-rear-cj5","transfer-case-cj5"],"summary":"Stock CJ-5 gearing was designed for 30-inch tires. Rolling up to 33s without a re-gear drops the effective crawl ratio by roughly 10% and puts additional strain on the already modest AMC drivetrain.","description":"The CJ-5's engines are not particularly powerful by modern standards. The AMC 258 inline-six produces about 110 hp in smog-era tune. The Buick 225 V6 (1965\u20131971) makes similar power. Neither engine has reserve torque to compensate for the tall-effective-gearing caused by larger tires. When you upsize from 30s to 33s without re-gearing, the driveline has to work harder through the entire rpm range \u2014 crawl ratio suffers, and the transfer case low-range multiplier is no longer delivering the mechanical advantage it was designed to.\n\nThe factory 3.54 and 3.73 ratios were calibrated for the 30x9.5 tires that came stock. For 33s, the math points to 4.10 gears. For 35s on a CJ-5 (a build that requires serious thought about roll cage and geometry \u2014 see `lift-springs-cj5`), 4.56 gears are appropriate.\n\n| Tire Size | Recommended Ratio | Notes |\n|-----------|------------------|-------|\n| 30x9.5 (stock) | 3.54 or 3.73 | factory spec |\n| 31x10.5 | 3.73 | manageable, mild |\n| 33x12.5 | 4.10 | minimum for daily use |\n| 33x12.5 trail only | 4.56 | better crawl, higher rpm highway |\n| 35x12.5 | 4.56 | with cage and full build |\n\nBoth axles must be re-geared to match. Running different ratios front and rear on an open differential is manageable but causes handling anomalies in 4WD.\n\nThe AMC 258 inline-six has more low-end torque than the Buick 225 V6 and tolerates modest under-gearing better. A 258-powered CJ-5 on 33s with 3.73 gears is unpleasant but functional. A 225 V6 on 33s with 3.73 gears will labor. If your CJ-5 has the V6 and you're going to 33s, 4.10 gears are not optional.\n\nDifferential setup \u2014 pinion depth, bearing preload, backlash \u2014 is a precision skill. A poorly set ring-and-pinion will whine, wear rapidly, or fail. If you have not set up a differential before, have a drivetrain shop do the install and limit your work to pulling and reinstalling the axle shafts. The ring-and-pinion itself is the cheap part; a botched install that destroys a $200 gear set requires another $200 gear set plus the rebuild parts. Shops typically charge $300\u2013500 per axle for re-gear labor."},{"id":"transfer-case-cj5","title":"Dana 20 Transfer Case \u2014 Service and Upgrade \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transfer-case","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":680},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","transmission jack","torque wrench","snap ring pliers","bearing driver set","drain pans"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1972-1983"],"tags":["drivetrain","transfer-case","dana-20","sye","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Dana 20 rebuild kit (bearings, seals, gaskets)","vendor":"Novak Conversions","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Dana 20 SYE (slip yoke eliminator) kit","vendor":"Novak Conversions","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Rear CV driveshaft (custom, CJ-5 with SYE)","vendor":"Tom Woods Custom Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Dana 18 rebuild kit (early CJ-5)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"Dana 20 Transfer Case Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/dana20/"},{"title":"SYE and CV Driveshaft Tech \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/sye-cj/"}],"alternatives":["regear-cj5","lockers-cj5"],"summary":"The Dana 20 is a robust part-time transfer case \u2014 the most common failure is worn output shaft bearings that create a howl that riders attribute to other causes for too long.","description":"Early CJ-5s (roughly 1966\u20131971) came with the Dana 18 transfer case. The Dana 20 was introduced in 1972 and continued through the end of CJ-5 production in 1983. They are different units with different rebuild parts, different mount patterns, and different low-range ratios. The Dana 20's 2.03:1 low range is the relevant spec for this entry; the Dana 18 is 2.46:1 (actually better crawl ratio). Both are covered by Novak Conversions and Omix-ADA rebuild parts.\n\nThe most common Dana 20 problem on an aged CJ-5 is worn output shaft bearings. The rear output shaft bearing in particular takes a beating from the constant torque cycling of trail driving. A worn bearing produces a howl or rumble under load that changes pitch with speed \u2014 it's often misdiagnosed as a differential or tire noise. Pull the cover and inspect the rear output bearing before attributing a driveline noise to something more expensive.\n\nThe input shaft seal and rear output shaft seal are also common leakers. A Dana 20 that drips gear oil onto the floor of the garage has a seal problem, not a structural failure. Rebuild kits from Novak Conversions ($95) include all bearings, seals, and gaskets.\n\nAt stock height, the Dana 20's slip yoke rear output works fine. With 2.5 inches or more of lift, the rear driveshaft angle steepens enough to create vibration at highway speeds. A slip yoke eliminator (SYE) converts the Dana 20 rear output from a slip yoke to a fixed flange, allowing the use of a double-cardan (CV) rear driveshaft that tolerates steeper angles.\n\nThe Novak SYE kit ($340) is the standard solution. It requires milling the rear output shaft and installing a new stub shaft and flange \u2014 a job for a machine shop or a shop with a lathe, not a driveway. The SYE is paired with a custom CV rear driveshaft from Tom Woods or a similar driveshaft shop ($280 custom-measured).\n\nIf your CJ-5 has 4 inches or more of lift and you're experiencing driveshaft vibration above 45 mph, the SYE plus CV shaft is the correct fix. A worn U-joint is the other possible cause \u2014 inspect and replace the rear U-joint before spending on an SYE."},{"id":"battery-cj5","title":"Battery Upgrade and Relocation \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"electrical","subcategory":"battery","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":380},"tools_required":["socket set","wire crimper","multimeter","battery terminal brush","drill with bits (if relocating)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["electrical","battery","relocation","optima","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Optima RedTop 34/78 battery (group 34)","vendor":"Optima Batteries","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Odyssey PC925 battery (group 25)","vendor":"Odyssey Battery","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Universal battery relocation tray (under-seat mount)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Battery cable upgrade kit (4-gauge, 4 ft)","vendor":"Waytek Wire","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Battery Relocation for Jeep CJ \u2014 Pirate4x4 Tech","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/battery-relocation-cj/"}],"alternatives":["wiring-harness-cj5","winch-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5's engine bay battery tray puts the battery where heat and vibration attack it hardest \u2014 and on a rig with a winch or auxiliary lights, the stock battery size is already inadequate.","description":"The CJ-5 factory battery tray sits in the engine bay, directly exposed to engine heat, splashing water, and the full vibration of the frame. Conventional lead-acid batteries tolerate vibration poorly \u2014 the plates loosen and shed material, reducing capacity and life. In an off-road application where the rig is being jostled constantly, battery life can be dramatically shorter than the manufacturer's rating.\n\nHeat is the other factor. Engine bay temperatures on a CJ-5 are harsh \u2014 the AMC 258 runs hot, and the CJ's minimal hood venting makes the engine bay a heat soak. High ambient temperature accelerates battery self-discharge and degradation.\n\nThe Optima RedTop ($190) and Odyssey PC925 ($215) are the two best choices. Both use spiral-wound AGM construction that is vibration-resistant and spill-proof. The Optima RedTop in Group 34 is the standard swap for a CJ-5 engine bay \u2014 it fits the factory tray dimensions and delivers better vibration resistance and cold cranking amps than a conventional flooded battery.\n\nThe Odyssey PC925 is slightly more compact and delivers exceptional pulse current \u2014 good for a rig with a winch where the battery must supply high amps for short periods without voltage drop.\n\nThe CJ-5 body has useful space under the driver's seat that can accommodate a Group 25 or smaller battery. This relocation serves two purposes: it moves the battery away from engine heat and into a slightly cooler, more protected location, and it shifts weight rearward on a rig that is slightly nose-heavy. A Rugged Ridge universal relocation tray ($55) mounts to the floor pan with four bolts.\n\nIf you relocate the battery, the positive cable run to the starter becomes significantly longer \u2014 plan for a new 4-gauge cable of the appropriate length (measure the run from the seat to the starter), and install an appropriately rated fuse within 18 inches of the battery. Do not run a longer cable on the stock-gauge wiring; voltage drop will cause starting problems."},{"id":"wiring-harness-cj5","title":"Wiring Harness Replacement \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"electrical","subcategory":"wiring","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":650},"tools_required":["wire stripper/crimper","multimeter","heat gun","electrical tape","zip ties","terminal puller","label maker or tape"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","basic electrical \u2014 reading wiring diagrams"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["electrical","wiring","harness","painless","fire-safety","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Painless Performance 18-circuit universal harness","vendor":"Painless Performance","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Omix-ADA CJ-5 replacement wiring harness (year-specific)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"Fusebox upgrade kit (blade fuse)","vendor":"Painless Performance","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Weatherpack connector assortment","vendor":"Molex / WiringPro","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Painless Performance Wiring Harness Installation Guide","url":"https://www.painlessperformance.com/instructions/"},{"title":"Jeep CJ Electrical System Overview \u2014 Omix-ADA Tech","url":"https://www.omix-ada.com/cj-electrical-tech"}],"alternatives":["battery-cj5"],"summary":"A factory wiring harness on a 1965\u20131983 CJ-5 has cracked insulation, corroded bullet connectors, and undersized wire by modern standards \u2014 it is a fire risk on a rig you're running hard off-road.","description":"The original wiring on a CJ-5 uses rubber-insulated wire that cracks and becomes brittle with age. Bullet connectors corrode in the presence of moisture, water, and salt. The factory fusebox used glass fuses in a fuse block that itself corrodes. Many CJ-5s have had decades of owner electrical modifications \u2014 added lights, stereos, CBs, GPS units \u2014 wired directly with terminal crimps and electrical tape, creating a patchwork that makes diagnosis nearly impossible.\n\nThe result is a rig that has gremlins: gauges that work intermittently, lights that flicker, a horn that sounds at random, engine that cuts out in heavy rain. These are not mechanical problems \u2014 they are the expected result of a 40\u201350 year old electrical system that was never comprehensively serviced.\n\nMore seriously, cracked insulation can arc against the frame or other wires, creating fire risk. An off-road CJ-5 vibrates more than a street car, and vibration accelerates insulation failure. A complete rewire is a significant investment of time but is one of the most reliability-improving modifications you can do on an old CJ.\n\nThe Omix-ADA harness ($285) is a direct OEM-replacement harness for specific CJ-5 years \u2014 it matches the factory routing, connector locations, and wire colors. If your CJ-5 is otherwise stock, this is the straightforward swap. Order the harness specific to your year; the harness changed between CJ-5 model years.\n\nThe Painless Performance 18-circuit universal harness ($420) is the choice for a modified CJ-5 \u2014 a rig with a different engine, added accessories, or a non-stock fusebox location. Painless harnesses come with labeled wires, modern blade fuses, and a comprehensive manual. The installation takes longer (plan two full days) but the result is a purpose-built, properly gauged electrical system with clear documentation.\n\nIf your CJ-5 still has points ignition, rewiring is the logical time to do the conversion to a Delco HEI electronic distributor. Points ignition requires regular adjustment and is vulnerable to moisture. An HEI conversion eliminates points completely. See `cj5-points-ignition` in the maintenance section for the distributor swap details."},{"id":"roll-cage-safety-cj5","title":"Roll Cage and Safety \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"interior","subcategory":"safety","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":60},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":3200},"tools_required":["MIG welder","angle grinder","notcher or cope saw","tube bender","drill press","measuring tape","level"],"skills_required":["welding","advanced fabrication \u2014 or hire a professional fabricator"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["safety","roll-cage","rops","interior","critical","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"GenRight Off Road CJ-5 ROPS bolt-in kit","vendor":"GenRight Off Road","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"DOM 1.75\" x 0.120\" roll cage tubing (20 ft)","vendor":"Metal Supermarkets","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Cage mounting floor plates (set of 4)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"Professional cage fabrication labor (local shop)","vendor":"Local fabricator","price_estimate_usd":1800}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ Roll Cage Safety \u2014 GenRight Off Road","url":"https://www.genright.com/cj-roll-cage-info"},{"title":"ROPS Standards for Off-Road Vehicles \u2014 SEMA","url":"https://www.sema.org/rops-standards"},{"title":"CJ-5 Rollover Dynamics \u2014 Pirate4x4 Technical Library","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/jeep-cj5-safety/"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj5","tire-sizing-cj5","lockers-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5 has no factory roll cage, no roll bar, and no side intrusion protection. On an 83.5-inch wheelbase with any lift, this is a genuine safety issue that deserves to be addressed before any other modification.","description":"When Jeep built the CJ-5, roll protection was not a priority. The factory rig came with a folding windshield and an optional soft top \u2014 there was no structural roll bar in production. A rolled CJ-5 without a cage exposes the driver and passenger to direct contact with the ground. On a rig this small, this compact, and this prone to rollover due to its short wheelbase and high center of gravity, the absence of roll protection is dangerous.\n\nThis is not a hypothetical concern. CJ-5s are known rollovers in the off-road community. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration investigated the CJ-5's stability in the late 1970s and early 1980s following a series of documented rollover fatalities. Jeep discontinued the CJ-5 in 1983 partly in response to these concerns. If you are lifting a CJ-5, running 33-inch tires, adding lockers, or driving on technical off-camber terrain, you need roll protection.\n\nA ROPS (roll-over protection structure) is the minimum acceptable protection for a trail-driven CJ-5. A basic ROPS is a single hoop behind the seats that mounts to the floor pan with welded-in plate anchors. This protects the driver's head space in a sideways roll. GenRight Off Road makes a CJ-5-specific bolt-in ROPS kit ($850) that requires no welding for installation \u2014 appropriate for a CJ-5 without full cage that sees moderate trail use.\n\nA basic ROPS is not a full cage. It provides single-axis protection in a direct roll but offers limited protection in a more complex tumble. For any CJ-5 run on technical terrain, a full cage is the right specification.\n\nA full cage on a CJ-5 includes: main hoop, front hoop or A-pillar bars, door bars on both sides, a rear diagonal from the main hoop to the frame, and floor plate anchors welded through the floor to backing plates on the frame rail. The cage must be built from properly sized DOM or HREW tube \u2014 1.75-inch x 0.120-inch wall is the standard specification for a CJ-5's weight class.\n\nFull cage fabrication is a job for an experienced tube fabricator. Pre-bent cage kits from GenRight, TNT Custom Steel, or other shops reduce fabrication time and ensure the geometry is correct. Plan for 20\u201360 hours of fabrication and welding. Budget $1,800\u2013$3,200 for professional fabrication. The difference between a correctly built cage and a tack-welded approximation can be the difference between surviving a rollover and not.\n\nThe correct build sequence for a CJ-5 trail rig is: (1) roll cage or ROPS, (2) lift and tires, (3) lockers. Not the reverse. Installing a 4-inch lift and 33-inch tires on a CJ-5 without roll protection first means driving a significantly more rollover-prone rig with no structural protection while you save up for a cage. Install the cage first."},{"id":"cj5-coolant-flush","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 Buick V6 and AMC Sixes","vehicle":"cj5","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":90},"tools_required":["drain pan (3 gal)","5/8\" hose","garden hose","funnel","needle-nose pliers (hose clamps)","3/8\" ratchet"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1971","1972-1983"],"tags":["cooling","fluids","maintenance","amc-258","buick-225"],"parts":[{"name":"Stant 195\u00b0F thermostat (AMC sixes)","part_number":"13808","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Thermostat housing gasket","part_number":"varies","vendor":"Fel-Pro","price_estimate_usd":6},{"name":"Prestone Long Life 50/50 (1 gal)","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Stant 16 PSI radiator cap","part_number":"10227","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"Cooling System Specs \u2014 CJ-5 (Kaiser Willys)","url":"https://www.kaiserwillys.com/tech-guide/55-75-willys-cj-5/cooling-system-specs-cj-5/"},{"title":"How much coolant? (Jeep-CJ forum)","url":"https://jeep-cj.com/community/threads/how-much-coolant.61071/"},{"title":"Novak Guide to Cooling for Jeeps","url":"http://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cooling"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"AMC 258 holds 10.5 quarts of coolant; Buick 225 V6 is close to 11 quarts including the heater core. Flush every two years or 30,000 miles. Stant 195\u00b0F thermostat and 16 PSI cap are the no-think replacements.","description":"The CJ-5's cooling system is small, exposed, and works harder than the radiator on any modern truck. Air flow through the grille is poor at low speeds, the engine bay traps heat, and there's no fan shroud on a lot of years. Coolant degrades \u2014 the corrosion inhibitors deplete in two to three years and the system starts eating itself from the inside, even if the coolant still looks green. The fix is a flush every two years and a thermostat swap every other flush.\n\nCapacity matters because the AMC 258 takes 10.5 quarts (about 2.6 gallons) of 50/50 mix to fully refill, and the Buick 225 Dauntless V6 is roughly the same. AMC V8 (304) variants run higher \u2014 about 14 quarts. The numbers include the radiator, block, heads, heater core, and hoses. Drain the radiator alone and you've only removed maybe 60% of the coolant \u2014 the block holds the rest until you pull the block drain plug.\n\nThe block drain plug on the AMC 258 lives on the driver's side of the block, above the oil pan rail, near the freeze plugs. It's a square-drive plug \u2014 3/8\" or 1/2\" ratchet drive goes straight into the head. The Buick 225 has block drains on both sides. They are universally rusted shut. Plan to soak them with PB Blaster the night before, and be ready to break one off \u2014 most CJ-5 owners replace them with brass plugs on first removal.\n\nThermostat spec is 195\u00b0F for both engines (180\u00b0F if you live somewhere hot and run a stock radiator that struggles \u2014 Phoenix in August, for example). Normal operating range is 195\u2013220\u00b0F. The radiator cap is 16 PSI; if yours is older than five years, replace it. A weak cap won't hold pressure and the coolant will boil at the block.","steps":["Engine fully cold. Pop the radiator cap. Position the drain pan under the radiator petcock (driver-side bottom tank).","Open the petcock and let the radiator drain. Two to three gallons should come out.","Move the drain pan under the block drain plug. Hit it with PB Blaster, then break it loose with a 3/8\" ratchet. Coolant will run for another minute or two.","Reinstall the block drain plug (replace with a brass plug if the OEM was rusted). Snug, not over-tight \u2014 these threads are soft cast iron.","Pull the upper radiator hose at the thermostat housing. Remove the housing (two bolts) and pull the old thermostat. Note the orientation \u2014 spring side faces the block.","Drop the new thermostat in with a fresh gasket. Reinstall the housing with even torque on both bolts.","Run a garden hose into the radiator filler neck and let water flow through with the petcock open until the water runs clear (5\u201310 minutes).","Close the petcock. Pour in 5 quarts of straight antifreeze, then top off with distilled water (or pre-mixed 50/50). For the 258, you'll use roughly 10.5 quarts total. Fill the radiator to the neck.","Reinstall the cap one click (not fully sealed) and start the engine with the heater on full hot. Let it warm to operating temp \u2014 the thermostat will open and the system will burp air.","Top off the radiator as the air burps out. Once stable, fully seat the cap. Check the overflow bottle level. Drive 10 miles, recheck cold the next morning, top off again."],"pros":["Two-year service that catches scale and rust before they kill the radiator","Cheap \u2014 under $50 for coolant, thermostat, gasket, and cap","Confirms the block drain plug isn't fused (find out now, not on the trail)"],"cons":["Block drain plug is almost always seized \u2014 budget for breaking one","Doesn't fully replace heater-core coolant unless you flush that separately","Mixing colors of coolant (orange OAT vs. green IAT) can cause sludge \u2014 pick one and stick with it"]},{"id":"cj5-grease-zerks","title":"Chassis Lube \u2014 Every Zerk Location","vehicle":"cj5","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":35},"tools_required":["grease gun","shop rags","needle-tip adapter (axle U-joints)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1955-1971","1972-1983"],"tags":["maintenance","lubrication","preventive"],"parts":[{"name":"Lucas Red 'N Tacky grease cartridge","part_number":"10005","vendor":"Lucas Oil","price_estimate_usd":6},{"name":"Mobil 1 Synthetic Grease cartridge","vendor":"Mobil","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Lock-N-Lube grease coupler","vendor":"Lock-N-Lube","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Grease zerk diagram (JeepForum)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/grease-zerk-diagram.1792537/"},{"title":"CJ Tune Up \u2014 How Many Grease Points? (CJ-8 forum)","url":"https://www.cj-8.com/threads/cj-tune-up-how-many-grease-points.34555/"},{"title":"Steering Knuckle Lubrication on CJ3B.info","url":"https://cj3b.info/Tech/SteeringKnuckles.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Every 3,000 miles or after every water crossing: tie rod ends (2), drag link ends (2), front driveshaft U-joints (2), rear driveshaft U-joints (2), front and rear slip yokes (2), and the steering bell crank if equipped (1). Eleven shots total. Five minutes with a grease gun.","description":"The CJ-5 chassis is built around grease-able joints \u2014 almost everything that pivots has a zerk fitting in it. Modern Jeeps have moved to sealed bearings and non-greaseable joints; the CJ-5 has not. Keep the grease fresh and the joints last decades. Skip it and they wear out in two seasons.\n\nThe full count depends on year and options. A 1976 CJ-5 with manual steering has roughly 11 zerks. With power steering, you lose the bell crank (3 zerks dropped) but gain the steering box if it's set up for it. Add a CV-jointed front driveshaft and you've added another zerk at the CV center. Some rebuilt steering knuckles include grease fittings for the kingpins or trunnions \u2014 those add four more shots.\n\nLocations to hit, working front to back:\n\nThe steering links \u2014 the long drag link (running from pitman arm to driver-side knuckle) has a zerk at each end. The tie rod (running across both knuckles, behind the axle) has a zerk at each end. Four shots total at the steering, more if the bell crank is still in service.\n\nFront driveshaft \u2014 one zerk on each U-joint (front of shaft and rear of shaft), one on the slip yoke (the splined section that telescopes). Three shots. Rear driveshaft is the same \u2014 three more shots. The slip yoke zerks often sit at awkward angles; a flexible grease-gun hose helps.\n\nFront axle U-joints (inside the steering knuckles) \u2014 these are the inner CV-style U-joints that let the front wheels turn while still spinning. Most stock axles do NOT have zerks here; if yours does (aftermarket Spicer or rebuilt with greaseable U-joints), use a needle-tip adapter and put one pump in each side. Over-grease them and the joint will blow a boot.\n\nDon't over-grease anything. Two to four pumps per zerk on the steering and driveshafts is enough. You want to see fresh grease start to emerge from the joint seal, not a fountain. Excess grease ends up on the trail and is wasted, except on the slip yokes \u2014 those benefit from being packed full because the shaft cycles inside.\n\nLucas Red 'N Tacky in a standard 14-oz tube is the working person's grease \u2014 sticky enough that it doesn't fling off, cheap enough that you'll actually do the service. Mobil 1 Synthetic is better for the front axle U-joints if the rig sees extreme heat or deep water.","steps":["Drive the rig until the front end and driveshafts are warm \u2014 old grease flows out more manageable when warm.","Park on level ground, set the parking brake, chock a rear wheel. Front end on the ground is fine for most zerks.","Crawl under with the grease gun. Start at the front driveshaft. Hit both U-joints (one at each end) and the slip yoke.","Move to the rear driveshaft. Same three zerks.","Move to the front axle. Find the tie-rod ends (both sides, behind the axle, under the housing). Two shots each.","Find the drag link ends \u2014 one at the pitman arm (driver side, near the steering box), one at the driver-side knuckle. Two shots each.","If equipped with a manual-steering bell crank (under the driver-side frame rail, near the steering box), hit the bell crank pivot zerk. One shot.","Wipe each zerk with a rag after grease starts to seep. Old grease and dust mixed equals abrasive paste \u2014 clean it off.","Inspect the boots on any greaseable joint. Torn boots mean the joint is already contaminated \u2014 grease won't save it."],"pros":["Five-minute job that extends the life of every steering and driveline joint","Catches torn boots and worn joints early","Free after the cost of a grease gun"],"cons":["Front axle U-joints with zerks can blow boots if over-greased","Sealed/non-greaseable U-joints in many rebuilds skip this step entirely","Doesn't address ball joints (kingpins on early CJs need teardown service, not a grease shot)"]},{"id":"cj5-oil-change","title":"Oil Change \u2014 Buick 225 V6 and AMC 258 I6","vehicle":"cj5","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":75},"tools_required":["3/4\" socket or wrench (drain plug)","oil filter wrench","drain pan (6+ qt)","funnel","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1971","1972-1983"],"tags":["fluids","maintenance","buick-v6","amc-258"],"parts":[{"name":"Wix 51515 oil filter (AMC 258 / AMC 304)","part_number":"51515","vendor":"Wix","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Wix 51258 oil filter (Buick 225 Dauntless)","part_number":"51258","vendor":"Wix","price_estimate_usd":11},{"name":"Valvoline VR1 Racing 10W-30 (high zinc, flat-tappet safe)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Rotella T4 15W-40 (high ZDDP, flat-tappet safe)","vendor":"Shell","price_estimate_usd":6}],"citations":[{"title":"Lubrication Specs \u2014 CJ-5 (Kaiser Willys)","url":"https://www.kaiserwillys.com/tech-guide/55-75-willys-cj-5/lubrication-specs-cj-5/"},{"title":"Jeepster 53-71 Service Manual (Buick 225)","url":"https://oljeep.com/53CjDj/53-71JeepCjDjServiceManual.pdf"},{"title":"AMC 258 oil capacity discussion (CJ-8 forum)","url":"https://www.cj-8.com/threads/258-i-6-oil-capacity.8276/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Buick 225 Dauntless takes 5 quarts with filter; AMC 258 takes 6 quarts with filter. Both are flat-tappet engines and need a modern oil with adequate zinc (ZDDP) \u2014 Rotella T4 15W-40 or Valvoline VR1 are the safe defaults.","description":"The CJ-5 ran three very different engines across its production: the Hurricane F4 four (1955\u20131971), the Buick 225 Dauntless V6 (1965\u20131971), the AMC 232 inline-six (1972), and the AMC 258 inline-six (1972\u20131983), plus an AMC 304 V8 in some years. Oil-change procedure is identical across all of them. Capacities are the part that catches people out.\n\nThe Buick 225 takes five quarts of oil with a filter change \u2014 that's straight from the factory Jeepster service manual. The AMC 258 is closer to six quarts with the filter installed, though sources vary slightly. Some forums say \"five plus one for the filter,\" others measure six. Trust the dipstick after the change, not the spec sheet \u2014 different aftermarket filters hold different amounts of oil and you can be a half-quart off either way without realizing it.\n\nOil weight: both engines are flat-tappet designs (no roller lifters), which means they need a modern oil with adequate zinc and phosphorus (ZDDP) to prevent camshaft and lifter wear. Most off-the-shelf passenger-car oils since 2007 have reduced ZDDP to protect catalytic converters \u2014 fine for modern engines, bad for an AMC 258. Use Rotella T4 15W-40 (diesel oil, high ZDDP), Valvoline VR1 Racing 10W-30 or 20W-50, or a dedicated classic-car oil. Skip plain 5W-30 off the Walmart shelf.\n\nThe oil filter on the Buick 225 is a Wix 51258 (or equivalent). The AMC 258 takes a Wix 51515 \u2014 the same filter the 4.0L XJ uses, which makes finding one trivial at any parts store. Hand-tight is the spec for both; an oil-filter wrench is for removal, not installation.","steps":["Warm the engine for five minutes so the oil drains freely. Park on level ground and set the parking brake.","Locate the oil drain plug on the oil pan (3/4\" hex on both engines). Position the drain pan, crack the plug loose with a wrench, and back it out by hand.","Let the pan drain ten minutes \u2014 until it's down to a slow drip.","Reinstall the drain plug with a fresh crush washer if the original is flattened or corroded. Torque is around 25 ft-lb on both engines; snug, not gorilla-tight.","Move the drain pan under the oil filter. Spin the filter off (counterclockwise from below). It will spill \u2014 expect that.","Wipe the filter mounting surface clean. Smear fresh oil on the new filter's rubber gasket. Spin the new filter on by hand until the gasket touches, then 3/4 to one full turn more.","Fill with the correct amount of oil through the valve cover or oil-fill cap. Start with 4.5 qt (225) or 5.5 qt (258), then top off based on the dipstick reading.","Start the engine. The oil-pressure light should go out within 5 seconds. Check the filter and drain plug for leaks.","Shut the engine off, wait 5 minutes for the oil to drain back to the pan, and check the dipstick again. Top off to the full mark."],"pros":["30-minute job with hand tools","Same procedure across both engine families","Filter for the AMC 258 is identical to the XJ 4.0L \u2014 straightforward to find anywhere"],"cons":["Both engines are flat-tappet \u2014 wrong oil will eat the cam","Capacity specs vary slightly by source; trust the dipstick, not the spec","1965\u201371 Buick 225 oil filter is harder to find than the AMC unit"]},{"id":"cj5-points-ignition","title":"Points Ignition Service \u2014 Dwell, Gap, Timing","vehicle":"cj5","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["timing light (inductive)","dwell meter or feeler gauge (0.016\")","distributor wrench","screwdriver set","10mm socket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","understanding ignition timing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1971","1972-1977"],"tags":["ignition","tune-up","points","amc-258","buick-225"],"parts":[{"name":"Standard Motor Products points set (Prestolite distributor, AMC)","part_number":"AL-22X","vendor":"Standard","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Standard ignition condenser","part_number":"AL-130","vendor":"Standard","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Distributor cap (AMC 258 Prestolite)","part_number":"AL-110","vendor":"Standard","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Rotor (AMC 258 Prestolite)","part_number":"DR-330","vendor":"Standard","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"74 CJ5 AMC 258 Timing and Points adjustment (JeepForum)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/74-cj5-amc-258-timing-and-points-adjustment.4400097/"},{"title":"HEI Ignition Upgrade for the AMC 258","url":"http://keith.cj8.tripod.com/hei_upgrade.htm"},{"title":"258 Ignition timing help (EarlyCJ5)","url":"http://www.earlycj5.com/xf_cj5/index.php?threads%2F51535%2F="}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Points gap is 0.016 inch (32 degrees dwell on a meter); timing on the AMC 258 is 8 degrees BTDC at 700 RPM with the vacuum advance disconnected and plugged. A dwell meter is more accurate than a feeler gauge \u2014 buy one before doing this job twice.","description":"Points ignition is what almost every CJ-5 came with from the factory before the HEI/electronic conversion options of the late 70s. The system is mechanical: a set of contact points opens and closes as the distributor shaft spins, breaking the primary circuit of the coil and inducing the high-voltage secondary that fires the plug. Points wear, the gap drifts, and timing wanders with it. A points tune-up is a 60-to-90-minute job that should happen every 12,000 miles or once a year \u2014 whichever comes first.\n\nThe spec for points gap on the AMC 258 Prestolite distributor is 0.016 inches. That's not a feeler-gauge spec you eyeball \u2014 it's a dwell-meter spec, because the angle the points stay closed (dwell) is what actually determines when the coil saturates. 0.016\" of gap corresponds to roughly 32 degrees of dwell on a six-cylinder. If you don't have a dwell meter, a feeler gauge gets you in the ballpark \u2014 start there, then drive to a parts store with a meter and confirm.\n\nTiming on a 1972\u201377 AMC 258 in a CJ-5 is 8 degrees BTDC (before top dead center) for 49-state trucks and 6 degrees BTDC for California-emissions trucks. Set it at idle \u2014 about 700 RPM with the choke fully open and the engine fully warm. The vacuum advance hose has to come off the distributor and get plugged with a vacuum cap or a golf tee, or you'll be chasing your tail.\n\nThe Buick 225 Dauntless V6 (1965\u201371) used a Prestolite IBS-4001 distributor with similar 0.016\" point gap. Timing on the 225 is 5 degrees BTDC at idle. Same general procedure, different spec.\n\nMany owners eventually swap to GM HEI or a Pertronix Ignitor electronic conversion. HEI gives a hotter spark, eliminates the points entirely, and survives the kind of dust and water you find on a trail. If you find yourself doing this job twice a year, the conversion pays for itself in time and reliability.","steps":["Warm the engine fully, then shut it off. Pop the distributor cap (two clips on the Prestolite).","Pull the rotor straight up. Inspect for cracks or burned tip \u2014 replace if either is true.","Loosen the screw on the points hold-down. Pull the old points and condenser out together. Note wire routing \u2014 take a photo if your memory is short.","Install the new condenser and new points. Don't touch the contact faces with your fingers; skin oil burns the points on first fire-up.","Bump the engine over with the starter (or by hand on the harmonic balancer bolt) until one of the distributor cam lobes is at its peak \u2014 the points should be at maximum gap.","Loosen the points adjustment screw. Slip a 0.016\" feeler gauge between the contacts. Adjust until the feeler has slight drag. Tighten the screw and recheck.","Reinstall the rotor and cap. Pull the vacuum advance line off the distributor and plug it.","Start the engine, let it idle at 700 RPM. Connect a timing light to the #1 plug wire and aim it at the harmonic balancer timing mark.","Loosen the distributor hold-down bolt (1/2\" wrench at the base). Rotate the distributor slowly \u2014 clockwise retards, counterclockwise advances on AMC sixes \u2014 until the timing mark lines up with 8 BTDC.","Snug the hold-down bolt. Reconnect the vacuum advance. Confirm timing didn't move when you tightened down."],"pros":["Cheap parts \u2014 full tune-up kit under $60","All hand tools, no specialty gear besides a timing light","Teaches you how ignition timing actually works"],"cons":["Points wear constantly \u2014 they'll need re-adjustment in 12,000 miles","Damp conditions cause misfires; trail water crossings are unkind","HEI or Pertronix conversion eliminates this whole job for under $200"]},{"id":"recovery-gear-cj5","title":"Recovery Gear for the CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"recovery","subcategory":"gear","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":520},"tools_required":["none \u2014 this is gear selection"],"skills_required":["basic recovery technique"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["recovery","snatch-strap","hi-lift","traction-boards","shackles","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB 20-foot kinetic snatch strap (17,600 lb)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"VIAIR 3/4\" recovery shackles (pair, rated)","vendor":"VIAIR / generic rated shackle","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"MAXTRAX traction boards (pair)","vendor":"MAXTRAX","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Hi-Lift Jack 48-inch (original)","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Hi-Lift base plate (soft terrain plate)","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"Off-Road Recovery Techniques \u2014 ARB Tech","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/arb-recovery-gear/"},{"title":"Hi-Lift Jack Safety and Use Guide","url":"https://www.hi-lift.com/instructions"}],"alternatives":["winch-cj5"],"summary":"At roughly 3,000 lbs, the CJ-5 is light enough to pull out of most situations with a snatch strap and a recovery buddy \u2014 but it also gets stuck in places a JK would never go.","description":"The CJ-5's short wheelbase allows it to fit terrain that defeats longer rigs. The consequence is that you'll high-center the belly, wedge the frame on a rock ledge, or drop a wheel into a hole that you couldn't see from the driver's seat. The short wheelbase means both axles can be on obstacles simultaneously \u2014 a condition that a longer rig would bridge. Recovery gear for a CJ-5 needs to account for these specific failure modes.\n\nA 20-foot kinetic snatch strap (17,600 lb MBS rating, one-third the vehicle weight for a dynamic pull) is the baseline. The CJ-5's low weight means a strap pull is usually effective \u2014 you don't need a full kinetic energy transfer strap as urgently as a 6,000-lb truck does, but the kinetic (stretchy) design is gentler on both vehicles than a static tow strap. Use rated recovery shackles (3/4-inch, 4.75-ton) at both ends \u2014 never use a hardware store shackle for recovery.\n\nTraction boards (MAXTRAX or knockoff equivalent) are the right tool for a CJ-5 stuck in mud, sand, or loose terrain. Slide them under the spinning tires, drive slowly onto the boards, and the rig typically walks itself free. On a lightweight CJ-5, one board per axle is usually enough \u2014 unlike heavier rigs that require two per tire. Carry two boards minimum.\n\nThe Hi-Lift jack is essential recovery equipment on a CJ-5 \u2014 more so than on a modern Wrangler. The CJ-5 frequently high-centers on rocks with both axles off the ground simultaneously. Getting a wheel back onto traction requires lifting the rig from the frame and placing a rock or traction board underneath. The Hi-Lift's ability to jack from very low heights and provide high lift is specifically what the CJ needs.\n\nUse the Hi-Lift on the frame or a dedicated receiver-mount \u2014 not on the factory bumper, which may not be strong enough for a full Hi-Lift load. The Hi-Lift base plate ($22) prevents the jack from sinking into soft ground. Follow the Hi-Lift safety protocol: always have someone steady the rig, keep clear of the lift path, and use the jack's handle lock. A loaded Hi-Lift drops catastrophically if the handle slips."},{"id":"winch-cj5","title":"Winch Selection and Frame Mounting \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":480,"high":1100},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill with bits","wire crimper","multimeter"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery","synthetic-rope","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn M8000 winch (8,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Superwinch Talon 9.5i (9,500 lb)","vendor":"Superwinch","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Synthetic winch rope 3/8\" x 50' replacement","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Winch remote controller relocation kit","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Frame rail gusset plates (pair)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"Winch Sizing and Selection Guide \u2014 Warn Industries","url":"https://www.warn.com/winch-guide"},{"title":"CJ Frame Gusset Tech \u2014 RuffStuff Specialties","url":"https://www.ruffstuffspecialties.com/cj-frame-support"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-cj5","recovery-gear-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5's thin frame rails are the limiting factor on a winch installation \u2014 a proper gusset across both rails is not optional if you're installing a winch-capable bumper and expect to use the winch under full load.","description":"The general rule for winch sizing is 1.5x the vehicle's gross weight. A CJ-5 runs 2,800\u20133,200 lbs depending on build, putting the target at 4,200\u20134,800 lbs minimum \u2014 but a 6,000 lb winch is the practical floor for a working trail rig because single-line pulls through soft terrain or up steep grades load the winch close to its rating. An 8,000\u20139,500 lb winch gives you meaningful headroom.\n\nThe Warn M8000 ($650) is the well-proven mid-size choice \u2014 reliable, serviceable, and appropriate for the CJ-5's weight. The Superwinch Talon 9.5i ($520) delivers 9,500 lbs at a lower price point \u2014 competitive internals and a good value for a CJ build. Do not spec a massive 12,000 lb truck winch on a CJ-5 \u2014 you'll exceed the frame's capacity to mount it, add unnecessary weight to the already-critical front end, and have more winch than the rig needs.\n\nThe CJ-5's frame rails at the front bumper mount are thin \u2014 the original steel was designed for the modest loads of a light 4x4, not a full-load winch pull against a tree. A winch in a self-recovery scenario can generate loads approaching 2x the rated capacity (friction, angle, soft ground). That load path goes directly through the bumper mount bolts into the thin frame rail flanges.\n\nWeld or bolt a gusset plate across both frame rails before installing a winch-capable bumper. RuffStuff Specialties makes bolt-on gussets for CJ frames ($85/pair) \u2014 no welding required. If you have welding access, a plate across the top of both rails tied into the front cross-member is a stronger solution.\n\nUse synthetic rope on a CJ-5. A snapped steel cable stores enormous energy and releases it violently \u2014 in the small cab of a CJ-5, a recoiling cable is much more dangerous than in a larger rig with more driver separation from the winch. Synthetic rope is safer (it drops when it breaks, not recoils), lighter, and easier to handle. If your winch came with steel cable, replace it with 3/8-inch synthetic ($95 from Warn).\n\nMount the handheld winch controller inside the cab where you can operate the winch from a seated position. The CJ-5 cab is tight, but a dashboard bracket or A-pillar clip keeps the controller accessible. Operating a winch while standing in front of the rig puts you in the line of a synthetic rope break \u2014 control from inside the cab whenever possible."},{"id":"steering-box-cj5","title":"Steering Box Upgrade \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"steering","subcategory":"steering-box","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":520},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","steering wheel puller","pitman arm puller","line wrench set"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["steering","steering-box","saginaw","borgeson","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Borgeson 800 series steering box (CJ-5 replacement)","vendor":"Borgeson","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Rebuilt Saginaw 800 steering box (CJ-7/XJ donor)","vendor":"NAPA Reman or local rebuild","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"AGR Rock Ram steering box (CJ)","vendor":"AGR Performance Steering","price_estimate_usd":495},{"name":"Steering box mounting gasket and hardware kit","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep CJ Steering Box Options \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-steering.htm"},{"title":"Borgeson CJ Steering Box Application Guide","url":"https://www.borgeson.com/application/jeep-cj/"}],"alternatives":["tie-rod-drag-link-cj5","lockers-cj5"],"summary":"A CJ-5 with a worn steering box isn't just imprecise \u2014 on a rig with manual steering, every worn joint telegraphs directly into the wheel, and a tired box on a locked CJ-5 makes trail driving genuinely dangerous.","description":"The CJ-5 came with either a Ross cam-and-lever steering box (very early units) or a Saginaw recirculating ball box. Both are manual steering \u2014 no power assist. In a manual steering system, there is no hydraulic damping to mask worn joints. Every bit of play in the steering box, every loose tie rod end, every worn drag link joint shows up at the steering wheel immediately.\n\nAfter 40+ years of use and weathering, the Saginaw 800 box on a CJ-5 almost certainly has more than 2\u20133 degrees of steering wheel lash. This translates to noticeable wandering on the highway and unpredictable steering response on the trail. With a front locker installed, the forces on the steering system increase significantly \u2014 a worn box becomes a safety issue.\n\nA rebuilt Saginaw 800 box from a 1975\u20131979 CJ-7 or a late XJ Cherokee is a cost-effective upgrade if you can find a low-mileage or professionally rebuilt unit. NAPA and rebuilder shops stock CJ-compatible Saginaw units for around $220. Confirm the input shaft diameter and mount bolt pattern matches your CJ-5 frame (they should match across most 1970s CJ production).\n\nThe Borgeson new-manufacture unit ($480) is the premium bolt-in option. Borgeson builds a variable-ratio recirculating ball box designed for the CJ \u2014 it's tighter on-center, has better feel at full lock, and comes with a new warranty. For a built CJ-5 that sees regular trail use, the Borgeson is worth the premium over a rebuilt unit of unknown history.\n\nAGR Performance Steering's Rock Ram ($495) is a high-effort, high-precision box designed specifically for trail use. Better feedback and more effort than the Borgeson \u2014 right for a dedicated rock crawler, possibly fatiguing for a rig that sees more highway than trail.\n\nSwap the steering box with the wheels straight ahead and the pitman arm indexed correctly \u2014 misindexing the pitman arm by a spline will cause full lock on one side and not the other. Use a pitman arm puller to remove the old arm; do not hammer it off the sector shaft. Torque the pitman arm nut to 180 ft-lbs \u2014 this is a high-torque fastener and must not be under-tightened."},{"id":"tie-rod-drag-link-cj5","title":"Tie Rod and Drag Link Replacement \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"steering","subcategory":"linkage","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":580},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","pickle fork or ball joint separator","pipe wrench","alignment gauge or string"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["steering","tie-rod","drag-link","linkage","death-wobble","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"TNT Customs HD tie rod and drag link (CJ-5)","vendor":"TNT Customs","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Synergy Mfg heavy duty drag link (CJ-5)","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Currie Enterprises DOM tube tie rod kit (CJ)","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Replacement tie rod ends (set of 4)","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ Steering Linkage Upgrade Guide \u2014 Four Wheeler","url":"https://www.fourwheeler.com/how-to/cj-steering-linkage-upgrade/"},{"title":"Death Wobble Diagnosis \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-tech/death-wobble-diagnosis.htm"}],"alternatives":["steering-box-cj5","lockers-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5's steering linkage is all below the frame, fully exposed to rock contact. A bent drag link from a trail hit causes immediate, dangerous steering problems \u2014 this is not a cosmetic issue.","description":"The factory tie rod and drag link on a CJ-5 are thin-wall stamped steel with replaceable tie rod ends. They were designed for a light off-road rig on mild terrain. Under trail use \u2014 particularly rock contact on technical terrain \u2014 the thin-wall tubes bend. A bent drag link changes toe on one side, creating a pull or a shimmy. A severely bent drag link binds in the steering geometry and causes unpredictable steering response.\n\nOn a CJ-5, the drag link runs from the steering box pitman arm forward to the driver's side knuckle, sitting low and exposed. Even a rock that doesn't feel like a major hit can be enough to indent or bend the tube. Worn or loose tie rod ends compound the problem \u2014 a loose end plus a slightly bent tube is the classic cause of the death-wobble oscillation that plagues old CJs.\n\nTNT Customs builds CJ-5-specific tie rod and drag link sets from DOM (drawn over mandrel) steel tube in 1.25-inch or larger outer diameter \u2014 significantly stronger and more impact-resistant than the factory tubes. The price is around $380 for the complete set. Synergy Manufacturing makes a standalone heavy-duty drag link for around $195 \u2014 if only one piece is worn, this is a cost-effective fix. Currie Enterprises offers a DOM kit that uses a slightly different tube geometry for improved strength.\n\nThese kits all use the same tie rod end dimensions as the factory components (either the factory taper or a modern replacement taper), so no special tools are required for installation beyond a ball joint separator.\n\nAny steering linkage work requires a toe alignment check afterward. The new tie rod links arrive at a set length that corresponds to rough alignment \u2014 you must measure and adjust toe to spec. The CJ-5 front toe spec is 1/8-inch toe-in. A string or tape-measure alignment in the driveway is accurate enough for a trail rig; a proper alignment rack is better. Do not drive on a freshly installed tie rod without checking toe \u2014 you will scrub through a set of tires in a few hundred miles with toe out of spec."},{"id":"leaf-spring-overhaul-cj5","title":"Leaf Spring Overhaul and Re-Arch \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"suspension","subcategory":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","torque wrench","C-clamp or spring vise","wire brush","rust penetrant"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["suspension","leaf-springs","overhaul","rebuild","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Spring eye bushing kit (front and rear, polyurethane)","vendor":"Energy Suspension","price_estimate_usd":48},{"name":"Leaf spring U-bolt kit (front and rear set)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Shackle rebuild kit (pair)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Re-arch service (per spring pack)","vendor":"Deaver Spring","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Leaf Spring Re-Arch and Rebuild \u2014 National Spring","url":"https://www.nationalspring.com/services/re-arching/"},{"title":"CJ Leaf Spring Bushing Replacement \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-leaf-springs.htm"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj5","shocks-cj5"],"summary":"A CJ-5 that sits nose-down or rides like a lumber wagon has almost certainly lost all of its spring arch \u2014 a 50-year-old leaf pack will sag flat without ever being overloaded.","description":"Leaf springs fatigue over time regardless of load. A CJ-5 that spent decades in a dry climate and was never overloaded will still have sagged springs after 40\u201350 years. The steel takes a permanent set under repeated flex cycles. When the arch goes flat, the spring rate drops, ride height drops, and the axle geometry shifts \u2014 caster angle and pinion angle both change when the spring arch collapses. This contributes to the death-wobble and vague steering feel that's common on uncared-for CJs.\n\nThe factory spring arch on a CJ-5 is modest \u2014 these were not high-travel springs to begin with. Restoring them to factory spec or slightly above gives noticeably better handling, improved ground clearance, and better axle geometry.\n\nFor a CJ-5 you're keeping close to stock, re-arching the existing springs is cost-effective if the leaves are in good condition \u2014 no cracks, no broken leaves, no severe corrosion. Deaver Spring, National Spring, and regional spring shops can re-arch a leaf pack for $100\u2013$150 per assembly. They'll also replace individual leaves if needed. This preserves the stock spring rate, which is appropriate for a CJ-5 used as a mild trail rig.\n\nIf the springs are cracked, broken, or heavily rusted, replace them. Omix-ADA stocks replacement factory-spec spring packs for most CJ-5 years ($120\u2013$180 per pack). Or use this as the opportunity to install an aftermarket lift kit \u2014 a fresh 2-inch lift kit from Rancho or Skyjacker starts around $280 and includes new shocks.\n\nThe spring eye bushings on a CJ-5 are pressed-in rubber or factory polyurethane. After 40 years, they are collapsed, cracked, or completely gone. Worn bushings allow the spring eye to clunk in the hanger bracket, accelerate wear on the hanger bolt, and contribute to imprecise handling. Replacing them with Energy Suspension polyurethane bushings ($48 for a full set) is a 30-minute job per corner once the spring is out. Polyurethane lasts far longer than rubber and doesn't require the regular lubrication that delrin bushings need.\n\nThe shackle bushings at the rear of the spring are equally important. Replace the shackle kit (bolts, bushings, nuts) while you have the spring out \u2014 the hardware is cheap ($28) and the shackle bolts on old CJs are frequently seized.\n\nWhen the axle comes down, inspect the U-bolts. A CJ-5 that's had its springs off before may have re-used U-bolts that are stretched. U-bolts are not reusable in the truest sense \u2014 they torque into a permanent stretch. If yours have any visible curve or the threads are damaged, replace them. Torque the U-bolt nuts to 45\u201355 ft-lbs (rear) and 35\u201345 ft-lbs (front), then re-torque after 50 miles."},{"id":"lift-springs-cj5","title":"Leaf Spring Lift Kit \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","breaker bar","spring compressor","pickle fork or ball joint separator"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","suspension basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["suspension","lift","leaf-springs","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Rancho RS6540 2\" lift spring kit (CJ-5)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Skyjacker C520 2\" leaf spring kit (CJ-5)","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Rough Country 4\" CJ-5 lift kit with springs and shocks","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Poly spring eye bushings (set of 8)","vendor":"Energy Suspension","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-5 Suspension Lift Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-suspension-lift.htm"},{"title":"Jeep CJ Spring-Over and Lift Overview \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cj-suspension/"}],"alternatives":["shocks-cj5","leaf-spring-overhaul-cj5","roll-cage-safety-cj5"],"summary":"On a CJ-5's 83.5-inch wheelbase, every inch of lift raises the center of gravity on a platform that was already tippy from the factory. Lift thoughtfully or cage first.","description":"Most lift kit marketing talks about ground clearance and tire fitment. On a CJ-5, those are secondary concerns. The 83.5-inch wheelbase is the shortest of any production CJ \u2014 shorter than the CJ-7 by 10 inches, shorter than a TJ by 10 inches, shorter than a JK by over 15. A lifted CJ-5 carries its raised center of gravity on a very short platform, which dramatically lowers the tipping threshold on off-camber terrain.\n\nAt stock height, the CJ-5 is already prone to rollover in situations a longer-wheelbase Wrangler would handle without drama. A 2-inch lift is manageable for an experienced driver on well-chosen terrain. A 4-inch lift on a CJ-5 without a roll cage is a serious safety choice that requires honest self-assessment. A spring-over axle (SOA) build \u2014 which effectively delivers 5\u20136 inches \u2014 belongs on a dedicated rock crawler with a full cage and a skilled driver, full stop.\n\nFor a CJ-5 used on trails, forest roads, and moderate rock, 2\u20132.5 inches of lift is the right call. It moves you from 30x9.5 tires to 31x10.5 with minor trimming at the front apron. It gives you meaningful ground clearance without dramatically worsening the already-marginal rollover dynamics.\n\nRancho and Skyjacker both make CJ-5-specific leaf spring kits in this range. Rough Country offers an entry-level kit. Replace the spring eye bushings while you have everything apart \u2014 the factory rubber bushings in a 40+ year old CJ-5 are almost certainly cracked or collapsed, and worn bushings create the vague \"floaty\" steering feeling that plagues neglected CJs. Energy Suspension polyurethane bushings ($45 a set) last far longer and don't require grease.\n\nA 4-inch lift on a CJ-5 can be done responsibly, but it requires a different set of commitments. A full roll cage or ROPS must be in place before you drive a 4-inch-lifted CJ-5 off pavement \u2014 the rollover risk is not theoretical, it is a documented failure mode of this platform with this geometry. See `roll-cage-safety-cj5` for cage options.\n\nAt 4 inches, the rear driveshaft angle on a Dana 20 or Dana 18 transfer case becomes steep enough to cause vibration and U-joint wear. A rear SYE (slip yoke eliminator) and a rear CV driveshaft are strongly recommended. Novak Conversions makes SYE kits for both the Dana 18 and Dana 20.\n\nThe leaf spring swap itself is a half-day job for a competent home mechanic. The front spring mount points are at the frame rail \u2014 the front hanger is forward of the axle, the rear shackle is aft. Rear springs follow the same layout. The CJ-5 leaf spring U-bolts should be replaced any time the axle comes down; they are torqued and re-torqued, but 40-year-old U-bolts stretch and can loosen on trail. Torque spec is 35\u201345 ft-lbs on the front spring hardware and 45\u201355 ft-lbs on the rear U-bolt nuts, though confirm with your kit's documentation.\n\nDo not skip the torque-after-driving step: tighten all suspension hardware again after the first 50\u2013100 miles on the new springs, once the bushings have seated."},{"id":"shocks-cj5","title":"Shock Absorber Replacement and Upgrade \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":480},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","open-end wrenches","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5100 shock set (CJ-5 front pair)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X shock set (CJ-5 4-pack)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series shock (CJ-5, each)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-5 Shock Absorber Fitment \u2014 Bilstein","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/en/us/fitmentguide/"},{"title":"Jeep CJ Suspension Travel and Shock Selection \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-shocks.htm"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj5","leaf-spring-overhaul-cj5"],"summary":"The most common shock installation mistake on a CJ-5 is ordering shocks for a lifted rig and bolting them onto stock springs \u2014 the shock overextends on droop and you've destroyed a $130 shock in a week.","description":"The CJ-5 uses simple twin-tube, bolt-in shocks front and rear. There is no crossover with TJ or JK shocks \u2014 the CJ-5 has a shorter wheelbase, shorter suspension travel, and different mounting geometry. The eye-to-eye (compressed) and extended lengths are what matter, and they must match your spring height, not some other CJ or Wrangler.\n\nBefore ordering shocks, measure the existing shock eye-to-eye length at full compression (axle up, weight on the spring) and at full droop (axle hanging, supported only by the limiting strap or shock itself). The new shock must have the same or marginally longer extended length (1/2 inch over stock droop is acceptable). A shock that is 1.5 inches too long will overextend every time the axle droops and will blow the shaft seal within a few hundred miles.\n\nIf your CJ-5 has a lift already installed, you will almost certainly need extended-travel shocks. Most lift kit manufacturers publish recommended shock lengths for their spring kits \u2014 use those specs, or measure.\n\nThe Bilstein 5100 is the clear choice for a street/trail CJ-5 \u2014 well-made, consistent quality control, and available in CJ-specific lengths. Bilstein's fitment guide lists CJ-5 applications by year and lift height. The Rancho RS5000X is a solid mid-price option and comes in 4-packs that give you front and rear in one order. Fox 2.0 Performance Series shocks are a step up in trail performance and appropriate for a dedicated trail build.\n\nFor a dedicated rock crawler on a built CJ-5, a bypass shock or remote-reservoir unit (Fox 2.0 Remote, King OEM Performance) is overkill unless the build is truly high-travel with an SOA or long-arm conversion.\n\nThe CJ-5 shock mount is a simple through-bolt at both ends \u2014 an upper mount to the frame or a frame bracket, and a lower mount to the axle perch. Torque the mounting nuts to 30\u201340 ft-lbs front and rear. On well-used CJ-5s the rubber shock bushings in the original mounts are shot; replace them with poly bushings when swapping shocks. A worn bushing lets the shock eye rattle and wear out the mounting bolt hole.\n\nDo the rear shocks with the axle at full droop to make sure the installed extended length is correct. Jack up just the frame, leave the axle hanging, and attempt to install the lower bolt. If you cannot reach the lower mount without compressing the shock, you have a shock that is too long and will overextend in use."},{"id":"tire-sizing-cj5","title":"Tire Fitment and Sizing \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire-sizing","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":480,"high":1200},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["tires","fitment","lift","sizing","rollover-risk","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 31x10.5R15","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Goodyear Wrangler MT/R 33x12.5R15","vendor":"Goodyear","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Baja Boss M/T 32x11.5R15","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":240}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-5 Tire and Lift Fitment Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-tires.htm"},{"title":"CJ-5 Rollover Risk and Stability \u2014 Pirate4x4 Safety","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/jeep-cj-rollover/"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj5","roll-cage-safety-cj5","wheels-cj5"],"summary":"Every tire size chart for the CJ-5 should start with this: the 83.5-inch wheelbase means that tire size is a rollover question first and a fitment question second.","description":"On a modern Wrangler JL or JK, tire size is largely a matter of lift height and fender clearance. On a CJ-5, tire size directly affects the rollover threshold in a way that is not trivial. Taller and wider tires raise the effective center of gravity, increase the rotational moment during cornering, and on an 83.5-inch wheelbase \u2014 the shortest production CJ \u2014 these factors compound quickly.\n\nA 33-inch tire on a 4-inch lift puts a CJ-5 at genuine rollover risk on moderate off-camber terrain without a roll cage. This is not a theoretical concern. CJ-5s have killed drivers on terrain that a longer-wheelbase Wrangler would handle without incident. Be honest with yourself about your driving, your terrain, and your cage status before choosing tire size.\n\n| Lift Height | Max Practical Tire | Notes |\n|------------|-------------------|-------|\n| Stock (0\") | 30x9.5 | factory spec, no trimming |\n| 1\u20131.5\" (shackles) | 30x9.5 to 31x9.5 | no trimming needed |\n| 2\" lift | 31x10.5 | minor front apron trimming |\n| 3\" lift | 32x11.5 | more apron trimming, reasonable CG |\n| 4\" lift | 33x12.5 | cage required, tube fenders |\n| SOA / 5\u20136\" | 33\u201335\" | full cage mandatory, experienced driver |\n\nThe 32x11.5 on a 3-inch lift is the practical limit for a CJ-5 without a roll cage being driven on mixed terrain by a thoughtful driver. Going larger requires an honest safety conversation about cage installation first.\n\nEven modest tire upsizing requires fender trimming on the CJ-5. The front apron (the lower front fender lip) is the first contact point during full steering lock. At 31x10.5, light trimming of the apron with a die grinder or tin snips clears it. At 32x11.5, more significant trimming or a slight flare is needed. At 33x12.5, tube fenders are the right call \u2014 the stock fenders are not wide enough and trimming them that aggressively weakens the structure.\n\nFor trail use, the BFGoodrich KO2 in 31x10.5 is the best all-around choice \u2014 aggressive enough for serious trail, livable on pavement, widely available. For a dedicated trail rig at 32 or 33 inches, the Mickey Thompson Baja Boss or Goodyear Wrangler MT/R are both well-regarded. Avoid ultra-wide mud tires (13.5\" section or wider) on a CJ-5 \u2014 the width increases the lever arm during off-camber maneuvers."},{"id":"wheels-cj5","title":"Wheel Selection \u2014 CJ-5","vehicle":"cj5","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":240,"high":680},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1965-1983"],"tags":["wheels","bolt-pattern","5x5.5","fitment","cj5"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Series 97 15x8\" steel wheel (5x5.5)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":68},{"name":"Black Rock Type 8 15x8\" steel wheel (5x5.5)","vendor":"Black Rock Wheels","price_estimate_usd":62},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Classic III 15x8\" alloy (5x5.5)","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep CJ Bolt Pattern Specifications \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj-wheel-guide.htm"}],"alternatives":["tire-sizing-cj5"],"summary":"The CJ-5's 5x5.5-inch bolt pattern (5x139.7mm) is shared with other older Jeeps and some trucks, but it is NOT the same as TJ, JK, or JL Wranglers \u2014 don't assume wheels from a later Jeep will fit.","description":"The CJ-5 uses a 5x5.5-inch (5x139.7mm) bolt pattern. This is a different spec than the TJ Wrangler (5x4.5\"), the JK (5x5\"), and the JL (5x5\"). If you are buying wheels for a CJ-5, confirm 5x5.5 before ordering \u2014 online fitment guides occasionally confuse CJ bolt patterns, and it is a frustrating way to buy the wrong wheels.\n\nThe 5x5.5 pattern is shared across older Dodge trucks, some Ford applications, and early Jeep products. This means wheel selection is good but it is not the same selection you'd find for a modern Wrangler.\n\nA 15x8-inch wheel with 3.75-inch backspacing is the standard setup for CJ-5 trail builds running 31\u201333 inch tires. The 3.75-inch backspacing positions the tire so it sits slightly outboard of the fender, giving clearance for the tire during full suspension travel without excessive poke that would make the rig wider than necessary.\n\nAvoid modern low-profile rims (17, 18, or 20 inches) on a CJ-5. Low-profile tires do not provide the sidewall flex that is essential for trail traction on a rock crawler. The 15-inch wheel with a tall-sidewall tire (75 or 80 series) gives you the sidewall compliance that makes the CJ-5's traction so effective on rock.\n\nSteel wheels are the right call for a CJ-5 used on trail. A bent steel wheel can be hammered back into shape trailside; a cracked alloy wheel cannot. Pro Comp and Black Rock both make 15x8-inch 5x5.5 steel wheels in the $60\u201370 range \u2014 inexpensive enough that you can carry a spare without regret. Alloy wheels look better and save a few pounds, but on a rig this old and this purpose-specific, steel is the practical choice.\n\nIf you run an alloy, keep a steel spare \u2014 the spare doesn't need to look good, just fit."}];